{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 20, :comments ["<b>On the day, with a hired razor.</b> A parable about the going forth of the angel of HaShem and the slaying of the camp of Assyria. Some interpret 'השכירה' as the great one, and they cite its parallel: 'also her hired ones are in her midst' (Jeremiah 46:21). In my opinion, השכירה is like 'בכח האכילה' (in the strength of that food) (1 Kings 19:8), and it derives from the root of שכר that the owner of the razor takes, because it was very sharp, for this is his craft. And the sense of 'the head and the beard' and 'prince and ruler' is as written (2 Chronicles 32:21):"], :book :isaiah, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 7, :notes {0 "Resolved השכירה as 'hired' (feminine adjective) per Ibn Ezra's own explanation of the root שכר; rendered the bare citation 2 Chronicles 32:21 though the verse content is not quoted."}, :source-witnesses [["Ibn Ezra on Isaiah, by M. Friedlander; Society of Hebrew Literature, London 1877" "https://www.nli.org.il/he/books/NNL_ALEPH001338443"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:52:42.040376Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}